# Add calibrated page marks and bilingual text layout diagnostics

## Scope and existing evidence

Implement seeded measured ruler marks, English/Spanish line breaking and
explicit missing-glyph warnings above the minimal bilingual inventory.

This is unfinished implementation work. The following original design
records remain accepted theoretical deliverables and are not reopened:

- ATR-P2-002 — archived semantic design
- ATR-P2-008 — archived semantic design
- ATR-P2-009 — archived semantic design

## Unicode whitespace in the native glyph coverage audit (partial)

The read-only `atrament-profiles glyphs --audit-text` previously counted
non-ASCII whitespace (including U+00A0, U+2009 and U+202F) as invisible,
reporting zero missing glyphs for samples that `notebook --place-text`
could not render. The native audit now skips only the compositor's implicit
ASCII space and structural control-character line breaks. Other Unicode
spaces retain their exact grapheme identities and report missing authored
strokes; there is no automatic ink, fallback or normalization.

Two native CLI regressions audit all three distinct spaces, verify that
ASCII spaces and source tabs/newlines do not inflate missing counts, and
confirm selected PDF and PNG outputs stay byte-identical when the native
compositor refuses unauthored Unicode ink. Explicitly authored versions
also produce real vector PDF strokes. This tightens diagnostics but does
not complete seeded ruler marks or bilingual line breaking in P2-013.

## Exact Unicode whitespace in statistics inventory (partial)

The separate `handwriting --inventory-text` diagnostic now uses the same
exact-ink whitespace distinction as the `glyphs --audit-text` command and
native flow compositor. Only implicit ASCII space and structural control
breaks are skipped; U+00A0, U+2009 and U+202F each appear as distinct
outside-baseline graphemes, retaining explicit per-glyph statistical
mutation labels. Unicode boundary traversal proceeds from remaining text
suffixes, avoiding repeated prefix scans on a bounded 4,095-byte example.

The Rust CLI regression exercises an override, repeated graphemes,
source tabs/newlines, a single Unicode-space input, and a long bounded
sequence. A further refusal case rejects text containing only implicit
ASCII spaces or source control characters, which previously produced a
misleading zero-grapheme report; one exact authored Unicode space remains
valid. This remains a read-only diagnostics report, not authored ink,
font generation, language-specific line breaking or a render guarantee.

## Single Unicode-space glyph audit admission (partial)

The reusable `glyphs --audit-text` diagnostic also admits input consisting
solely of an exact non-ASCII space glyph. Previously its all-whitespace
precheck refused such text even though a longer string including the same
glyph was auditable and native export accepted its authored strokes.
U+00A0, U+2009 and U+202F now each report their distinct missing or
explicitly authored pen shapes on their own. ASCII-space-only and
control-break-only sources still refuse; the audit remains read-only and
does not invent a font or replace a saved glyph library.

## Explicit structural breaks in coverage audits (partial) — 2026-11-04

The native `glyphs --audit-text` and `handwriting --inventory-text`
commands now reject vertical tab, form feed and U+0085 NEL even within
otherwise visible text. Those control breaks were previously admitted
as whitespace, then silently omitted from exact coverage reports, while
`notebook --place-text` refuses them outright. Both read-only diagnostics
continue accepting ASCII space, tabs, CRLF and LF as source structure and
report exact non-ASCII space glyphs separately. A focused regression
checks both refusal and the accepted CRLF/LF/tab control case.

A follow-up also rejects unpaired carriage returns at the start, middle
or end of otherwise visible audit text. The native Markdown/plain-text
flow importer already refuses bare CR, so both diagnostics now use the
same line-ending boundary. Paired CRLF and plain LF remain valid.

This is input-parity hardening, not general bilingual layout or printed marks.

## Unicode separators in glyph coverage (partial) — 2026-11-04

The read-only native `glyphs --audit-text` and
`handwriting --inventory-text` reports now recognize U+2028 line separators
and U+2029 paragraph separators as *structural*, matching the native
Markdown/plain-text flow importer. Previously both reports listed those
source row boundaries as outside-baseline or missing authored glyphs,
misleading users about a bilingual source's actual ink coverage.

One red-before/green-after Rust regression proves both reports exclude
those separators while keeping composed `ñ`, decomposed `n` plus tilde,
U+00A0 nonbreaking spaces and U+2009 thin spaces distinct. Purely
structural-only sources still refuse rather than reporting zero coverage.
The existing C0/C1, bare-CR and exact-Unicode admission rules remain in
place. This repairs coverage diagnostics; it does not implement a complete
semantic notebook, custom ruler sampling, or implicit glyph synthesis.

## Initial UTF-8 BOM in native coverage audits (partial) — 2026-11-04

The native read-only glyph and handwriting inventory reports now ignore
exactly one initial U+FEFF as source-file metadata, matching the bounded
Markdown/plain-text flow importer. Previously a BOM-prefixed bilingual
source reported a nonexistent missing ink shape and outside-baseline glyph;
BOM-only source was also incorrectly admitted as visible content.

A red-before/green-after native regression proves that an initial BOM and
Unicode line separator do not inflate two exact NFC/NFD text graphemes,
while a later U+FEFF remains a distinct auditable identity. Structural-only
sources refuse, and both commands still count the ignored BOM toward the
original 4,096-byte input cap. This does not add a font, synthesize ink, or
claim complete multilingual notebook acceptance.

## Structural Unicode separators cannot be pen glyphs (partial)

The native exact-glyph CLI now refuses U+2028 and U+2029 as authored glyph
stroke identities and as direct `--place-text` characters. These same
separators are structural line or paragraph boundaries in imported flow
and are intentionally omitted from read-only glyph inventory reports.
Previously explicit strokes could render ink for characters the coverage
audit ignored, yielding a false diagnostic-to-output correspondence.

A red-before/green-after Rust regression verifies both separators refuse,
with and without an explicitly authored separator shape, before replacing
an existing PDF. Other caller-authored Unicode spaces, including NBSP and
thin space, remain legitimate distinct glyphs. This is a local admission
consistency fix, not a complete language layout or font implementation.

